s say romance languages are spoken  by let's say 700 million people in the  world  germanic languages turkic languages  slavic languages there are these large  language families  in my own case i have learned five  romance languages five slavic languages  uh you know three or four germanic  languages and i could learn more of  more like dacha i could probably go  after a danish so if you learn one form  of the language it doesn't prevent you  from learning other forms in fact it  makes it easier  so  if i look at my  possible interaction with arabic  most of it is going to be reading  because i don't have that many arabic  speaking friends and i'm not about to  spend a lot of time in  saudi arabia or in  lebanon or in you know morocco  but i can always read al jazeera or some  other source  uh to get a sense of what's happening  and what arab speakers think or i can  watch  you know television or radio and things  of that nature  so it's more of a passive involvement  point number one point number two i have  to be able to read my way of learning is  very much dependent on reading i like  reading  and so in any case i have to get a good  grip on the arabic script  so i might just as well learn modern  standard arabic now  modern standard arabic is a bit like  latin it's sort of the the  it's the language of the quran just as  latin as the language or was the  language certainly of the catholic  church  um  it's sort of a base from which these  other  uh languages have sprung or maybe it's  just a form of the language  separate from the way that people speak  but it has the advantage of being  a common denominator wherever you go  apparently and i'm speaking from  ignorance just as we chose the flag from  ignorance  wherever you go in the arabic world  whatever you're going to read is going  to be in standard arabic chances are  that radio and television is going to be  in standard arabic at least some of it  will be so it to some extent it's a  lingua franca just like latin was a  lingua franca  now  i do want to learn  some of the versions of arabic at least  to be able to understand them  so for example at link where we are  increasing our arabic  uh content and i'll talk a bit about  that  uh we are also adding content in  levantine arabic and i hope to add some  content in egyptian arabic so that  people can also hear these other forms  and if they need  to work more on those forms of the  arabic language they can do so and i may  very well do so  in the future  so  and you know if we look at at a map of  where arabic is spoken  uh you know it's it's a language that as  i understand it from reading it came  from  saudi arabia or yemen or somewhere down  there and gradually moved up with the uh  advance of islam so much like  christianity and european languages were  spread by armies and explorers and  missionaries  some of the same happened with the  spread of the arabic language and islam  spreading as far  along the north african cross right to  southern spain  and going east you know quite a ways  and so that languages that were spoken  in these areas before like berber in  north africa or coptic in egypt or  aramaic  uh or syriac  in what is now sort of the levant  lebanon syria and so forth these were  gradually replaced by arabic  and this process of arab  by  arabicization has continued up until  recent times when berber or  assyrian or aramaic have been suppressed  in favor of arabic  and but the influence of these local  languages and the influence of  other colonizers such as french  colonizers or british colonizers  uh  have influenced the different regional  versions of arabic  so yes  i'm going to focus on modern standard  arabic  through my reading and listening  but i do eventually want to learn some  of the other varieties of arabic for  example i had intended to go to lebanon  because i'm going to visit my son and  his family in london over christmas and  we're gonna go early and i was gonna fly  to lebano